From 5365eb35dcb9dcc77cfe64f5d79518d9000f8d49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:40:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] support: add a kqueue io_broker backend for Darwin

The io_broker has one implementation, io_broker_epoll, built on epoll
and eventfd. Both are Linux-only, and every application and most gateway
tests create their broker through create_io_broker(io_broker_type::epoll),
so nothing in the tree could run on Darwin.

Add io_broker_kqueue with the same structure and the same public
behaviour. Every registered descriptor is an EVFILT_READ knote armed
with EV_ADD | EV_ENABLE | EV_DISPATCH, the kqueue equivalent of
EPOLLONESHOT: the knote is disabled once an event is delivered and
re-enabled with EV_ENABLE after the receive callback has run, so one
callback is in flight per descriptor. EV_EOF with no data left maps to
error_code::hang_up, EV_EOF with a socket error in fflags and EV_ERROR
map to error_code::error, and EV_EOF with data still readable is
delivered as data first, as EPOLLIN | EPOLLRDHUP is. The control channel
is a self-pipe instead of eventfd: enqueue_event() writes one byte to
the write end, the read end is registered like any other descriptor and
the handler drains it before popping the control queue. Registration,
deregistration (including from inside a callback), pending removals and
shutdown follow the epoll implementation line by line.

io_broker_type gains a kqueue value. On Darwin the factory serves both
epoll and kqueue requests with the new backend, so callers keep one
spelling; on Linux a kqueue request still terminates as unsupported.
lib/support compiles exactly one backend per platform.
---
